---
title: Modules and project structure
description: Add a second source file, import it, and understand the two project manifests.
sidebar:
  order: 4
---

`lui.json` describes your app. `LuiToolchain.json` selects the exact SDK used to
check and run it. Keep both files; editing an SDK pin is not a fix for an
undeclared source file.

## Add one module

In a basic project, create `Src/Greeting.luau`:

```luau
return {Text = "Hello from a module"}
```

Replace `Src/Main.luau`:

{{GlobalExample:require}}

Use this complete `lui.json` for those two files. Keep the SDK-created
LuiToolchain.json unchanged.

```json
{
  "SchemaVersion": 2,
  "Script": "Src/Main.luau",
  "Capabilities": [],
  "Extensions": [],
  "Assets": [],
  "ExtensionDeclarations": [],
  "Diagnostics": {"Mode": "Development"},
  "Modules": {
    "Root": "Src",
    "Files": ["Greeting.luau", "Main.luau"],
    "Requires": [],
    "Dependencies": []
  }
}
```

**Expected result:** checking succeeds and the app opens a window titled Hello
from a module. Main.luau loads the table returned by Greeting.luau.

## Names and paths

`Script` is relative to the project folder. `Modules.Files` is relative to
Modules.Root, so write `Greeting.luau`, not `Src/Greeting.luau`, in that list.
Spelling and letter case must match. `require("./Greeting")` is relative to the
source file making the call; it does not search your computer or download code.

For another new `.luau` file, save it under Modules.Root and use **LUI: Add File
to Project**. Review and save the proposed Modules.Files change, then check
types. Merely creating a file does not enroll it in checking or execution.

## Shared state and initialization

Within one app run, a module is loaded once and its non-nil result is reused.
Two screens importing the same state module therefore see the same table.
Restart starts fresh Luau state; this is not persistence to disk.

Return exports from a module, then start effects from app code after loading.
Do not make module imports wait on I/O or initialize circular imports. For
example, if A requires B and B requires A, change the design so they depend on a
third shared module instead.

## Optional library dependencies

An `@Library/Module` import uses a declared dependency alias, not a package
registry. Start with [the bundled library projects](../libraries/) so their
source files and content locks are already present. The
[advanced tutorial](../../learn/advanced-project/) builds up a modular app step
by step. The [project-host contract](https://github.com/gmoddev/LUI/blob/sdk-v0.1.0-preview.2/docs/PROJECT-HOST.md)
is deeper host-integration documentation, not a prerequisite for adding a module.
